  "summary": "A structural and spiritual architecture of pointed arches, rib vaults, flying buttresses and walls of stained-glass light.",
  "description": "Emerging at Saint-Denis under Abbot Suger, Gothic construction solved how to open stone walls to light: pointed arches, ribbed vaults and flying buttresses carried loads outward, allowing verticality and vast glazing. Regional variants — French Rayonnant and Flamboyant, English Perpendicular, German Sondergotik, Brick Gothic of the Baltic — flourished in cathedrals, universities and town halls. Revived in the 18th-19th centuries, it remains the archetype of upward-striving sacred space.",
